  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "The records establish several relevant building blocks: ink-extrusion printing with polymer binders followed by hydrogen treatment and sintering (R1), in-situ alloying of mixed powders into dense BCC refractory HEAs by additive manufacturing (R5, R7, R9, R11, R12), and quench-controlled retention of a BCC state in a closely related Nb-Ta-Ti-Zr refractory alloy (especially R8). They also provide general support for nonequilibrium phase selection, interstitial strengthening, and protective oxide films. However, none proposes a HfH2/NbH/TaH0.5/TiH2/ZrH2 ink, sequential hydride decomposition governed by formation enthalpies, deliberate oxygen uptake with a titanium getter, or a self-healing silicide coating. Thus the literature points in the right direction, but selecting hydride precursors and integrating the specific decomposition, oxygen-control, quenching, and coating mechanisms would require a substantial new idea."
